<DocsLayout title="Security" description="Security defaults and trust boundaries in Hologram Live.">
  <h1>Security</h1>
  <p>Loopback-only defaults, bounded inputs, explicit authorities, digest verification, typed configuration, and narrow process boundaries form the baseline.</p>

  <h2>Network defaults</h2>
  <ul>
    <li>The service binds to loopback by default.</li>
    <li>Non-loopback listeners require bearer-token authentication.</li>
    <li>Non-loopback remote clients require HTTPS.</li>
    <li>gRPC messages and HTTP bodies have independent size limits.</li>
  </ul>

  <h2>Fallback safety</h2>
  <p>Authorization denial, authentication failure, TLS validation failure, and integrity failure never fall through to another target. A mutation transport failure is reported as an unknown commit state instead of being retried elsewhere.</p>

  <h2>Content integrity</h2>
  <p>Objects are addressed with BLAKE3. Imported <code>.holo</code> archives are parsed by the pinned archive implementation and their footer fingerprint is verified before catalog admission.</p>

  <h2>Application capabilities</h2>
  <p>A <code>.holo</code> archive contains an untrusted capability request, never its own authority. Live requires canonical upstream <code>CapabilitySet</code> bytes and admits the decoded request against a host-supplied effective grant before provider preparation. The canonical empty set is the only deny-all representation; zero-byte and otherwise malformed objects fail closed. The default local grant has no storage roots, channels, or network endpoint scopes. Endpoint scopes are canonical HTTPS host, port, and path-prefix values; they attenuate for children but link no guest network interface in the current release. Insufficient authority returns <code>LIVE_AUTHORIZATION_DENIED</code>.</p>
  <p>A child edge's delegated capability object is also untrusted until admitted. Live requires the parent grant to admit that delegation and the delegation to admit the child's requested set. The same rule is applied transitively to nested children. Amplification and under-granted requests return <code>LIVE_AUTHORIZATION_DENIED</code> before the root or any child provider prepares.</p>
  <p><code>--development-grant</code> is restricted to direct local files. Resident development grants come only from trusted service configuration and require a loopback listener; remote callers cannot submit a grant. Capability decision traces contain identities, counts, scalar limits, and allow/deny outcomes, not raw documents or secrets. An awaited JSONL audit row records the principal, relation, application identities, request/delegation κ, grant κ, trusted source, and outcome before provider preparation. It excludes capability documents, roots, channels, tokens, headers, and payload bytes. Successful run and resident results carry only the non-secret request κ, grant κ, trusted-source label, and decision.</p>
  <p>Core-Wasm guest contract v1 has no imports or WASI. The manifest entry selects only a typed export inside the resolved module, never a provider or host function. Source schema v4 writes Wasm <code>contract</code> to canonical <code>aux</code>; exact contract lookup never falls back across identifiers. Component v1 executes through a separate import-free provider with runtime-owned 64 MiB memory, 100 million fuel, 1 MiB input/output, and two-second deadline ceilings. A fresh store isolates each input, while a component-local epoch interrupts timeout, stop, or dropped-future cancellation without affecting another application or core Wasm. Separate exact-object read, typed-graph read, object write, and channel publish/subscribe profiles link only their fixed mediated interface after the corresponding canonical fields are admitted. Typed-graph read preserves the same single read-only ABI but expands authority only under its distinct contract: Live re-hashes and resolves every locally reachable registered canonical UOR object before linking, rejects missing or malformed closure members without exposing κ values, and bounds depth, objects, edges, and aggregate bytes. Unknown types are opaque leaves, and exact-object read remains exact. Channel messages are capped at 64 KiB, FIFO mailboxes at 64 entries, and full publish rejects without overwrite; receive is nonblocking and at-most-once, with no waiter left by cancellation and no sockets, persistence, replay, acknowledgement, or broadcast. Future mediated network imports require their corresponding admitted fields; clocks, random, environment, process, secrets, inference, and raw sockets remain unavailable while the canonical capability schema cannot represent them. Under-granted imports fail before linker construction.</p>
  <p>Python uses that unchanged import-free Component world. Compilation runs Hologram's deterministic <code>componentize-py 0.25.0 --stub-wasi</code> through an isolated <code>uvx</code> environment with developer Python search paths removed. The compiler chooses an exact immutable Hologram release wheel URL/SHA-256 for each supported release host and disables indexes and source builds; unpinned hosts fail closed. External packages must be registry records with exact HTTPS, SHA-256-pinned Python 3 <code>*-none-any.whl</code> artifacts in <code>uv.lock</code>; uv installs only those direct URLs with indexes, dependency re-resolution, source builds, and cache links disabled. Nested source or installed symlinks, native payloads, and native/source-only locks fail closed with rootfs guidance. A versioned compile report records normalized source hashes, the componentizer patch contract and dependency artifacts, observed tools, target ABI, and output κ without embedding build-host evidence in canonical application identity. Stubbed randomness is deterministic build state, not a secure runtime random source; no WASI authority is linked into Live.</p>
  <p>Experimental Python rootfs checks report the requested base and whether it is digest-pinned, hashed source inputs, pinned uv, normalized archive contract, and source epoch without contacting a registry. Before a real build, Hologram resolves a mutable tag from Docker's raw schema-2 registry manifest, hashes the exact manifest bytes, and uses the resulting digest-qualified reference in <code>FROM</code>. Bundle schema 3 rejects unsafe or inconsistent save output and rewrites exact config/layer bytes into canonical SHA-256 blob paths with fixed manifest, tar, and compression encoding. Completed provenance records requested and resolved references plus observed Docker/image identities and output κ. These observations remain outside the archive. Digest binding closes the tag-movement race and canonical export removes Docker-save metadata variation; clean cross-host equality, SBOMs, and signed attestations remain open.</p>

  <h2>Tracing privacy</h2>
  <p>Tokens, authorization headers, raw prompts, response bodies, private keys, and uploaded bytes must not enter trace fields. OTLP export remains disabled until a collector endpoint is explicitly supplied.</p>

  <h2>Interactive API reference</h2>
  <p>Scalar's JavaScript is served from the Hologram binary. Its optional agent is disabled, and the reference reads the OpenAPI document from the same origin.</p>
